/// \defgroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries Script engine lookups, named reveals and Lua glue
/// \ingroup grp_scripting
/// \brief How map scripts turn names into units, object type lists, trigger
///        areas and players; the named permanent map reveals; the record of
///        acquired sciences; the chunk writer for action lists; and two small
///        Lua bindings.
///
/// Map scripts refer to everything by name: a unit by the script name the
/// map gives it, an area by its polygon trigger's name, an object type list
/// by its name, a player by name or, where the handler uses the
/// player-name resolver, by a selector such as `<This Player's Enemy>`. The
/// `ScriptActions` and `ScriptConditions` handlers resolve unit, object type
/// list and trigger-area names through the `ScriptEngine` lookups in this
/// group: ScriptEngine::getUnitNamed, ScriptEngine::didUnitExist,
/// ScriptEngine::getObjectTypes and
/// ScriptEngine::getQualifiedTriggerAreaByName.
/// ScriptEngine::getSkirmishEnemyPlayer answers the player-name resolver's
/// `<This Player's Enemy>` selector. Each returns null (or false) when
/// nothing matches, which the handlers generally treat as nothing to do or
/// as a false condition.
///
/// The group also holds ScriptEngine::doNamedMapReveal and
/// ScriptEngine::undoNamedMapReveal (permanent reveals that a script can
/// later undo by name), ScriptEngine::notifyOfAcquiredScience (the player
/// code telling the script engine about a science),
/// WriteActionDataChunk (how a script's actions are written as data chunks
/// in the map-file script format),
/// and two C functions the Lua engine registers: ObjectHasUpgrade and
/// `_ALERT`. \ref sub_scripting describes the script engine as a whole.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The names are data: unit names, trigger areas, waypoints, reveal names
///   and object type list names all come from the map and its scripts.
///   The unit, object type list and alias comparisons here are
///   case-sensitive, and reveal names are matched by a CRC of the exact
///   characters, so a name must be spelled exactly as the map defines it.
/// - The special names are hard-coded: `<This Object>` in the unit lookup,
///   the four `[Skirmish]...Perimeter` aliases in the trigger-area lookup,
///   and the player selectors in the player-name resolver.
/// - The five lookups (ScriptEngine::getUnitNamed,
///   ScriptEngine::didUnitExist, ScriptEngine::getObjectTypes,
///   ScriptEngine::getQualifiedTriggerAreaByName and
///   ScriptEngine::getSkirmishEnemyPlayer) are called from script actions,
///   script conditions and the AI player, so their answers must agree on
///   every machine. What their bodies read includes script-engine data,
///   the player list, the game logic's object table (through
///   GameLogic::findObjectByID) and terrain data; the trigger-area lookup's
///   debug warning also reads the logic frame counter (or a Living World
///   value), only to label the message for the script debug window. None
///   of them reads the local player or uses a random generator. (The
///   current enemy comes from a virtual call made through the player; what
///   that call reads is not established here.)
/// - The named reveals are different: their player name can be one of the
///   selectors `<Local Player>`, `<Local Player's Enemies>`,
///   `<Local Player's Allies>` or `<Local Player's Allies incl Self>`. The
///   player-name resolver answers these from the player list's local
///   player, so they resolve to a different player mask on each machine.


/// \fn Object *ScriptEngine::getUnitNamed(Parameter *p)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief Finds the game object that a unit parameter of a map-script action
///        or condition names.
///
/// This is the entry point behind almost every "named unit" script type.
/// More than a hundred action and condition handlers call it, for example
/// ScriptActions::doNamedAttackArea, ScriptActions::doNamedCustomColor,
/// ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedInsideArea and
/// ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedUnitDestroyed. The handlers pass the
/// script parameter itself, not just its text.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The lookup tries, in order:
/// 1. the object ID the parameter carries: when it is not zero and
///    GameLogic::findObjectByID still finds that object, that object is the
///    answer. Where the ID in a parameter is set is not established here.
/// 2. the parameter's name. An empty name gives null.
/// 3. an internal by-name helper. It answers `<This Object>` with the
///    engine's calling object, or its condition object when there is no
///    calling object. Any other name is first split into an optional
///    `prefix/` qualifier (up to the first `/`) and a bare name; an
///    unqualified name gets the engine's default prefix. The (prefix, bare
///    name) pair is looked up in a keyed table that maps names to object IDs
///    (Open-BFME-1's reconstruction of BFME 1 calls these unit references).
///    If the pair is in the table, the helper returns whatever
///    GameLogic::findObjectByID returns for the stored ID, which is null when
///    that object is gone; it does not search further. Only a name that is
///    not in the table is then looked for in the engine's list of named
///    objects, by exact comparison. For a qualified name that comparison
///    uses the bare name, because the split removes the prefix from the
///    helper's own copy of the name.
///
/// \param p  The script parameter that names the unit. Must not be null.
/// \return The object, or null when nothing matches. The object can already
///         be dying: ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedUnitDestroyed tests the
///         result with the object's effectively-dead check.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's ScriptEngine::getUnitNamed takes only the name string
///       and has no object-ID step; it handles `<This Object>` and then
///       scans the named-object list. BFME 1 already has the by-name
///       helper with the unit-reference table.
///
/// \par Modding
/// `<This Object>` is a hard-coded name. A unit name may be written as
/// `prefix/name`; a name without a `/` uses the engine's default prefix
/// (what sets that default is not established here). Names must match the
/// map's object names exactly, including case. A misspelled name is not an
/// error: the lookup returns null and the action or condition quietly does
/// nothing or is false.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The steps above are read from the matched BFME 2 body, the matched
/// by-name helper it calls and the matched name-qualifier split. The name
/// and the Parameter argument come from the ledger and from the callers,
/// which pass the parameter they read from the action. The unit-reference
/// term is BFME 1's.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::didUnitExist, GameLogic::findObjectByID,
///      ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedUnitDestroyed


/// \fn bool ScriptEngine::didUnitExist(const AsciiString &name)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief Tells whether a name belonged to an object that no longer exists.
///
/// The engine keeps a list of named objects: each record pairs a name with
/// its object. This function finds the record with exactly this name and
/// answers true when the record's object pointer is null, that is, when
/// the name was known but its object is gone. A live object, and a name the
/// engine never saw, both give false. In Zero Hour, destroying a named
/// object keeps its record and only clears the pointer, so that scripts can
/// still ask later whether the name ever existed. That BFME 2 clears records
/// the same way is an inference from this function and its callers.
///
/// Callers in BFME 2 include:
/// - ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedUnitDestroyed and
///   ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedUnitTotallyDead first call
///   ScriptEngine::getUnitNamed. When that finds no object, they answer with
///   this function, so `NAMED_DESTROYED` and `NAMED_TOTALLY_DEAD` are true
///   for a unit that existed and is gone, and false for an unknown name.
///   ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedUnitDying calls it too but answers false
///   either way, as in Zero Hour.
/// - object-creation script actions, when they give a new object a script
///   name. Two of them follow the same naming step as Zero Hour's
///   ScriptActions::doCreateObject and ScriptActions::createUnitOnTeamAt
///   (Zero Hour: when ScriptEngine::getUnitNamed still finds an object with
///   the name or this function answers true,
///   ScriptEngine::transferObjectName hands the name's record to the new
///   object; otherwise the object gets a new record). A third
///   unit-creation action makes the same choice on this function's answer
///   alone.
///
/// \param name  The script name to test. Compared exactly, including case.
/// \return True when a record with this name exists and its object pointer
///         is null; false otherwise.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Because an unknown name gives false, a typo in a unit name makes a
/// "destroyed" condition never fire rather than fail loudly.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Behaviour from the matched BFME 2 body, which matches Zero Hour's and
/// BFME 1's; the callers are matched rows, described here by role. The
/// record-clearing on destruction, ScriptEngine::transferObjectName and the
/// two creation-action names cited are Zero Hour's.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::getUnitNamed,
///      ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedUnitTotallyDead


/// \fn ObjectTypes *ScriptEngine::getObjectTypes(const AsciiString &objectTypeList)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief Finds a script object type list by its name.
///
/// An object type list is a named set of object types that actions and
/// conditions accept where they expect an object type. BFME 2's action
/// table has the `OBJECTLIST_ADDOBJECTTYPE` and
/// `OBJECTLIST_REMOVEOBJECTTYPE` actions, which in Zero Hour are what build
/// the lists. The engine keeps its lists in one list of `ObjectTypes`
/// pointers, which this function searches. Callers include
/// ScriptConditions::evaluatePlayerHasKilledTypeUnits and more than a dozen
/// script-action handlers.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A linear scan of the engine's list. Null entries are skipped, and the
/// first list whose name equals the argument is returned. The loop matches
/// Zero Hour's. Zero Hour's source also has a `DEBUG_CRASH` on a null
/// entry, but that macro compiles to nothing in release builds, so it is
/// not expected in a release body.
///
/// \param objectTypeList  The list name. Compared exactly, including case.
/// \return The list, or null when no list has that name.
///
/// \par Modding
/// In Zero Hour a list exists only while a script has created it: Zero
/// Hour creates the list on the first add (or remove) action for its name
/// and deletes it when it becomes empty; until then, and after that, an
/// action given the name finds nothing. Zero Hour's ScriptEngine::xfer
/// saves the lists through ObjectTypes::xfer. How BFME 2 creates and saves
/// the lists is not established here.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Behaviour from the matched BFME 2 body. The creation and deletion rules
/// (ScriptEngine::doObjectTypeListMaintenance there) and the saving of the
/// lists through ScriptEngine::xfer are Zero Hour's.
///
/// \see ObjectTypes::xfer, ScriptConditions::evaluatePlayerHasKilledTypeUnits


/// \fn Player *ScriptEngine::getSkirmishEnemyPlayer()
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief Picks the player that the current script player treats as its
///        enemy.
///
/// The player-name resolver calls it for the `<This Player's Enemy>`
/// selector, so a player parameter that a handler resolves through the
/// player-name resolver can mean "this player's enemy". Not every handler
/// does: at least one matched special-power script action looks its player
/// name up directly by name key in `ThePlayerList`, and for such a handler a
/// selector names no player. Other callers of this function include
/// AIPlayer::guardSupplyCenter and a team-movement script action.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - With no current player (the player whose scripts are running; see
///   ScriptEngine::getCurrentPlayer), the answer is null.
/// - Otherwise the current player's current enemy is returned, through the
///   player's current-enemy getter. Zero Hour reads that enemy from the
///   player's AI.
/// - When the player has no current enemy, the function returns the first
///   player in `ThePlayerList` whose player type is human, or null if there
///   is none.
///
/// \return The enemy player, or null.
///
/// \warning The player-name resolver uses the result for
///          `<This Player's Enemy>` without a null check.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version also skips the dummy player named
///       `ThePlayer` when the current campaign is a Generals Challenge
///       campaign; the BFME 2 body has no such check. Zero Hour's source
///       also has a `DEBUG_CRASH` for the case with no current player, but
///       that macro compiles to nothing in release builds, so its absence
///       from the BFME 2 release body is expected.
///
/// \par Modding
/// When the current player has no current enemy, the fallback is the first
/// human player in player-list order (in Zero Hour the current enemy is the
/// player's AI enemy). In a game with several humans, that is not
/// necessarily the nearest or the strongest one.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Behaviour from the matched BFME 2 body, which follows Zero Hour's
/// statement for statement apart from the Generals Challenge check noted
/// above (Zero Hour's debug-only check is compiled out of release builds).
/// The
/// `<This Player's Enemy>` caller is target evidence from the resolver's
/// body.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::getQualifiedTriggerAreaByName,
///      ScriptEngine::getCurrentPlayer, AIPlayer::guardSupplyCenter


/// \fn PolygonTrigger *ScriptEngine::getQualifiedTriggerAreaByName(AsciiString name)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief Finds a trigger area by name, first expanding the four skirmish
///        perimeter aliases.
///
/// Script types that take a trigger area resolve it here, for example
/// ScriptActions::doNamedAttackArea, ScriptActions::doTeamAttackArea,
/// ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedInsideArea,
/// ScriptConditions::evaluateTeamInsideAreaEntirely,
/// ScriptConditions::evaluateNamedEnteredArea,
/// ScriptConditions::evaluateSkirmishNamedAreaExists and the named map
/// reveals. The aliases let one skirmish AI script work on any map and from
/// any start position.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - `[Skirmish]MyInnerPerimeter` and `[Skirmish]MyOuterPerimeter` become
///   `InnerPerimeter` or `OuterPerimeter` followed by the current player's
///   multiplayer start index plus one (so `InnerPerimeter1` for the first
///   start position). With no current player the function returns null
///   without a lookup or a warning.
/// - `[Skirmish]EnemyInnerPerimeter` and `[Skirmish]EnemyOuterPerimeter` do
///   the same with the current player's current enemy. With no current
///   player or no enemy, the number becomes -1 (for example
///   `InnerPerimeter-1`), which normally names nothing, so the lookup below
///   fails.
/// - Any other name is used as given.
/// - TerrainLogic::getTriggerAreaByName then looks the name up. When it
///   finds nothing, the function sends "!!!WARNING!!! Trigger area 'name'
///   not found." to ScriptEngine::AppendDebugMessage with the pause flag
///   set. In BFME 2 that message reaches only the script debug window, when
///   its DLL is loaded and the lite debug mode is off, and the pause flag
///   is ignored.
///
/// The name is taken by value because the aliases rewrite it.
///
/// \param name  The trigger-area name or one of the four aliases.
/// \return The trigger area, or null.
///
/// \par Modding
/// For the aliases to work, a map defines polygon triggers named
/// `InnerPerimeter<n>` and `OuterPerimeter<n>`, numbered from 1 by start
/// position. The alias strings and the two base names are hard-coded. A
/// missing area is silent in a normal game; only the script debugger shows
/// the warning.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The matched BFME 2 body follows Zero Hour's function statement for
/// statement, with the same alias strings. The behaviour of
/// ScriptEngine::AppendDebugMessage is from its matched BFME 2 body.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::getSkirmishEnemyPlayer, ScriptEngine::doNamedMapReveal,
///      ScriptEngine::AppendDebugMessage, TerrainLogic::getTriggerAreaByName


/// \fn void ScriptEngine::doNamedMapReveal(const AsciiString &revealName)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief Reveals the shroud for a named permanent reveal: a radius around a
///        waypoint, or the cells of a trigger area's bounding rectangle that
///        pass a per-cell test supplied by the trigger.
///
/// The engine keeps a list of named reveal records. Each holds a CRC of the
/// reveal name, the name of a waypoint or trigger area, a radius, a player
/// name and a kind (0 for a waypoint, 1 for a trigger area). Its two
/// callers are short script-action handlers that first add a record and
/// then call this function with the same name: one passes a radius and kind
/// 0, the other a radius of zero and kind 1. Their parameter lists fit the
/// actions `MAP_REVEAL_PERMANENTLY_AT_WAYPOINT` and
/// `MAP_REVEAL_PERMANENTLY_IN_TRIGGER`, whose last parameter is the name
/// the reveal is "afterwards referred to as"; the handlers are paired with
/// those actions by matching parameter lists, which is an inference. The
/// record lets a later script undo exactly this reveal with
/// ScriptEngine::undoNamedMapReveal.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The function computes a CRC of the requested name and scans the list
///   for the first record with the same CRC. Only that record is used; with
///   no match nothing happens.
/// - The record's player name becomes a player mask through the engine's
///   player-name resolver. Besides player names it accepts selectors such
///   as `<This Player's Enemy>`, `<This Player's Allies>`,
///   `<Local Player>` and `<All Players>`, so one reveal can apply to
///   several players.
/// - Kind 0: TerrainLogic::getWaypointByName finds the waypoint, and the
///   shroud manager's radius reveal uncovers the record's radius around its
///   location.
/// - Kind 1: ScriptEngine::getQualifiedTriggerAreaByName finds the area (so
///   the skirmish perimeter aliases work here too). The shroud manager's
///   rectangle reveal receives the area's bounding rectangle and a
///   reference to an object inside the trigger. It walks the cells of the
///   rectangle and reveals a cell only when a per-cell test passes; that
///   test turns the cell into a world position and hands it to a virtual
///   function of the trigger's object. That this is the trigger's
///   point-in-polygon test is an inference, so whether cells outside the
///   polygon but inside its bounding rectangle are revealed is not
///   established; the per-cell test suggests they are not.
/// - A missing waypoint or area, or any other kind, reveals nothing.
///
/// The helper that adds a record first looks for one with the same name CRC
/// and, if there is one, keeps it unchanged. Reusing a reveal name before it
/// has been undone therefore repeats the first reveal, whatever the new
/// action's waypoint, area, radius or player. Zero Hour likewise refuses to
/// redefine a named reveal.
///
/// \param revealName  The reveal's name, as the creating action gave it.
///
/// \note Zero Hour and BFME 1 compare the reveal name as a string and
///       support waypoints only. Zero Hour also stops when the player name
///       does not resolve to a player. BFME 2 compares CRCs, so two reveal
///       names with the same CRC cannot be told apart; the first record
///       wins.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A reveal name is free text chosen in the map's script. It has to be
/// unique, and the undo action has to use exactly the same spelling,
/// including case. A `<Local Player...>` selector as the reveal's player
/// resolves against each machine's own local player, so the revealed
/// players differ from machine to machine.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Behaviour from the matched BFME 2 body, its matched callers and the
/// matched player-name resolver. The roles of the shroud calls (a radius
/// reveal, and a rectangle walk with a per-cell test that consults the
/// trigger) come from their matched bodies and the matched cell-walk and
/// cell-test bodies they use. That the per-cell test is a polygon test is
/// an inference.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::undoNamedMapReveal,
///      ScriptEngine::getQualifiedTriggerAreaByName,
///      TerrainLogic::getWaypointByName


/// \fn void ScriptEngine::undoNamedMapReveal(const AsciiString &revealName)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief Takes back a named permanent reveal: the inverse of
///        ScriptEngine::doNamedMapReveal.
///
/// Its caller is the handler that fits the undo-reveal actions
/// (`MAP_UNDO_REVEAL_PERMANENTLY_AT_WAYPOINT` and
/// `MAP_UNDO_REVEAL_PERMANENTLY_IN_TRIGGER` take only the reveal name; the
/// pairing is by matching parameter lists, as for the reveal). That handler
/// calls this function and then removes the record, so a reveal can be
/// undone once.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same lookup as ScriptEngine::doNamedMapReveal: the first record whose
/// CRC equals the CRC of the name, the player mask from the record's player
/// name, then the waypoint or the trigger area found again by name. The
/// shroud manager's matching undo operations are called with the same
/// arguments: the radius and location for a waypoint, or the bounding
/// rectangle and the reference into the trigger for an area, and the mask.
/// If the waypoint or area cannot be found any more, nothing is undone.
///
/// \param revealName  The name the reveal was created with.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The undo recomputes the area from the names at the time of the undo.
/// Renaming or removing the waypoint or trigger area between the reveal and
/// the undo leaves the reveal in place.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Behaviour from the matched BFME 2 body, which mirrors
/// ScriptEngine::doNamedMapReveal call for call, and from its matched
/// caller.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::doNamedMapReveal


/// \fn void ScriptEngine::notifyOfAcquiredScience(int playerIndex, ScienceType science)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief Records that a player has acquired a science, so that script
///        conditions can react to it.
///
/// The engine keeps one list of newly acquired sciences for each of its 20
/// player slots. This function appends the science to the list of the
/// given player index. ScriptEngine::reset clears all of them.
///
/// In BFME 2 it is called from the player's science code in two places:
/// the player's science reset, which rebuilds the player's sciences from
/// the player template and the rank starting sciences and then notifies
/// every science in the resulting list, and the player's add-science
/// function. Zero Hour's callers are the same pair, Player::resetSciences
/// and Player::addScience. In Zero Hour the condition code asks
/// ScriptEngine::isScienceAcquired (which can remove the entry it finds),
/// and ScriptEngine::xfer saves the lists.
///
/// \param playerIndex  The player's index in the player list. Not checked
///                     against the 20-slot bound.
/// \param science      The science acquired.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Sciences are defined in INI; this list only records which ones were
/// granted, for the script conditions to see.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Behaviour and the slot count from the matched BFME 2 body and
/// ScriptEngine::reset. The two call sites are read from the retail code,
/// and the science reset's behaviour from its matched body. The reader and
/// the saving are Zero Hour's.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::reset


/// \file GameLogic/ScriptEngine/ScriptActionWriteAction.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief The writer for a script's action lists (map-file script format),
///        WriteActionDataChunk.
///
/// WriteScriptDataChunk calls it for each non-empty action list (the true
/// list and the false list) of each script it writes.


/// \fn void WriteActionDataChunk(char *chunkName, DataChunkOutput &chunkWriter, ScriptAction *action)
/// \brief Writes a chain of script actions as data chunks in the map-file
///        script format, one chunk per action.
///
/// WriteScriptDataChunk calls it once for each non-empty action list of a
/// script: with the chunk name `ScriptAction` for the list run when the
/// script's conditions hold, and `ScriptActionFalse` for the list run when
/// they do not. An empty list writes no action chunk at all. On the
/// reading side, ScriptAction::ParseActionDataChunk and
/// ScriptAction::ParseActionFalseDataChunk are registered for those chunk
/// names and append each action they read to the script's matching list.
/// It writes the map-file script format. In Zero Hour this writer is
/// reached only from WorldBuilder: from its map save (through
/// SidesList::WriteSidesDataChunk) and from its script dialog's script
/// export (through ScriptList::WriteScriptsDataChunk), which saves a
/// separate `.scb` script file rather than a map. What reaches it in the
/// BFME 2 game executable is not established here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function follows the chain through each action's next pointer. For
/// each action it opens a chunk of version 3 under the given name and
/// writes, in order:
/// - the action type number;
/// - the name key of the type's internal name, taken from
///   ScriptEngine::getActionTemplate, or the key of `Bogus` when there is no
///   template (a fallback kept from the donor; ScriptEngine::getActionTemplate
///   returns entry 0 rather than null for an out-of-range type);
/// - the parameter count, then each parameter through
///   Parameter::WriteParameter;
/// - one more integer, holding a byte-sized field of the action whose
///   meaning is not established;
///
/// and then closes the chunk.
///
/// \param chunkName    The chunk name, `ScriptAction` or `ScriptActionFalse`.
/// \param chunkWriter  The output file.
/// \param action       The first action of the chain; null writes nothing.
///
/// \note Zero Hour and BFME 1 have two static members,
///       ScriptAction::WriteActionDataChunk and
///       ScriptAction::WriteActionFalseDataChunk, each with its chunk name
///       fixed, and write version 2 chunks without the trailing integer.
///       BFME 2 has one free function that takes the name, and version 3
///       adds the trailing value.
///
/// \par Modding
/// BFME 2's own writer emits version 3 action chunks with the trailing
/// value; whether its reader also accepts version 2 chunks is not
/// established here. In Zero Hour and BFME 1 the reader uses the stored
/// internal-name key to re-find the action type when the type numbers have
/// changed (see Template).
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Format from the matched BFME 2 body and its matched caller; the
/// two-member Zero Hour and BFME 1 layout and the name-key matching on load
/// from their Scripts.cpp.
///
/// \see Condition::WriteConditionDataChunk, Parameter::WriteParameter,
///      ScriptEngine::getActionTemplate, ScriptAction::ParseActionDataChunk


/// \file GameLogic/ScriptEngine/LuaObjectHasUpgrade.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief The Lua binding ObjectHasUpgrade, one of the C functions the Lua
///        engine makes available to `Scripts.lua`.


/// \fn int ObjectHasUpgrade(lua_State *L)
/// \brief The Lua function `ObjectHasUpgrade(object, upgradeName)`: whether
///        the object's controlling player has the named upgrade's bit set
///        (only for upgrades whose type field is 0; see below).
///
/// The Lua engine registers it as the global `ObjectHasUpgrade` in its
/// script state, the one that runs `Data\Scripts\Scripts.lua` and the
/// handlers named in `Data\Scripts\ScriptEvents.xml`.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - With fewer than two arguments it returns no value.
/// - Argument 1 gives an object ID through an internal Lua stack accessor.
///   When that yields zero and argument 1 is not nil, it returns no value;
///   otherwise GameLogic::findObjectByID looks the object up, and a missing
///   object returns no value.
/// - Argument 2 is the upgrade name, looked up with UpgradeCenter::findUpgrade
///   among the upgrades that `Upgrade` INI blocks define. An unknown name
///   returns no value.
/// - Otherwise it pushes one number: 1 when a type field of the upgrade
///   template is 0 and the object's controlling player has the upgrade's bit
///   set, and 0 in every other case.
///
/// In Zero Hour's layout of the upgrade template, that field is the upgrade
/// type, and 0 is the player-wide type. If BFME 2 keeps that layout, an
/// object-level upgrade always answers 0, even when the object has it. This
/// is an inference; the BFME 2 body shows only the test against 0.
///
/// \param L  The Lua state, with the object as argument 1 and the upgrade
///           name as argument 2.
/// \return The number of Lua results: 0 (the script sees nil) or 1.
///
/// \warning The body does not check the controlling player for null; an
///          object without one reaches the player's bit test with no player.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The result is the number 1 or 0, not a boolean. In Open-BFME-1's
/// reconstruction of EA's Lua fork only nil and false fail a test, so a 0
/// would pass an `if`; compare the result with 1. The upgrade name is the
/// INI block name.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Behaviour from the matched BFME 2 body; the registration from the matched
/// Lua registration code. The type-field reading is a Zero Hour layout
/// inference, and the truth rule is BFME 1's.
///
/// \see UpgradeCenter::findUpgrade, Object::getControllingPlayer,
///      GameLogic::findObjectByID


/// \file Common/LuaAlert.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_script_engine_queries
/// \brief The engine's Lua `_ALERT` hook, registered in both of the Lua
///        engine's states.


/// \fn int _ALERT(lua_State *L)
/// \brief The engine's replacement for Lua's `_ALERT` message hook. It
///        builds the message and then discards it.
///
/// Lua 4.0's libraries report errors by calling the Lua global `_ALERT`
/// with the message text: the base library's error handler and the I/O
/// library's error traceback both do, and the base library installs a
/// default `_ALERT` that writes to stderr. The Lua engine opens its states
/// with the standard libraries and then registers this function as
/// `_ALERT`, replacing that default. It does so for both of its Lua states:
/// the script state and the one used by the `CurDrawable` functions.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The body forms "LUA Alert: " in a local string and appends argument 1
/// when lua_tostring can convert it to text: a string, a number, or (in
/// EA's Lua fork) a boolean, which becomes `true` or `false`. Any other
/// argument adds nothing. It then returns no Lua results without passing
/// the string anywhere. Building a message
/// that is never used suggests a logging call removed from the release
/// build; that is an inference.
///
/// \param L  The Lua state; argument 1 is the message.
/// \return Always 0 (no Lua results).
///
/// \par Modding
/// Lua's own error reports end here and are dropped, and the engine's
/// script loading ignores the result of running a script file. Test Lua
/// changes where errors are visible.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Lua's own error reports end here and are dropped, and the script loader
/// ignores the load result; this is the natural place to restore Lua error
/// output, by forwarding the message to the engine's log or debug output.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Behaviour from the matched BFME 2 body; the registrations from the
/// matched Lua registration code; the library side, including
/// lua_tostring's conversion, from the matched Lua library units; the
/// ignored load result from the matched script-loading code.
///
/// \see ObjectHasUpgrade
